            "text": "{\"study_topic_id\":\"study-049868463ed0d8d0debb\",\"overview\":{\"text\":\"Two (2) is the second positive integer and the first prime number. It is even, but uniquely among primes: every prime other than 2 is odd. In geometry, two points on a line identify that line, which extends infinitely in both directions. The word “binomial” names a polynomial with exactly two terms, so 2 also appears in algebraic language. When a two-term expression such as x+y is raised to the nth power, its expansion has n+1 terms—not just two—and the binomial theorem gives the coefficients, which can be arranged in Pascal’s triangle. These properties make 2 distinctive in number theory, geometry, and algebra.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://mathworld.wolfram.com/2.html\",\"https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/10-1-points-lines-and-planes\",\"https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/10-1-add-and-subtract-polynomials\",\"https://openstax.org/books/precalculus-2e/pages/11-6-binomial-theorem\"]},\"key_facts\":[{\"text\":\"2 is the second positive integer and the first prime number.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://mathworld.wolfram.com/2.html\"]},{\"text\":\"2 is even and is the only even prime.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://mathworld.wolfram.com/2.html\"]},{\"text\":\"A binomial is a polynomial with exactly two terms.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/10-1-add-and-subtract-polynomials\"]},{\"text\":\"For a binomial raised to the nth power, the expansion has n+1 terms; Pascal’s triangle displays the coefficients.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://openstax.org/books/precalculus-2e/pages/11-6-binomial-theorem\"]},{\"text\":\"A line is described by two points on it and extends infinitely in both directions.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/10-1-points-lines-and-planes\"]}],\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"2\",\"url\":\"https://mathworld.wolfram.com/2.html\",\"publisher\":\"Wolfram MathWorld\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:41:57.993943+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Identifies 2 as the second positive integer, the first prime, and the only even prime.\"},{\"title\":\"10.1 Points, Lines, and Planes\",\"url\":\"https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/10-1-points-lines-and-planes\",\"publisher\":\"OpenStax\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:41:57.993943+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Explains how two points describe a line and that a line extends infinitely in both directions.\"},{\"title\":\"10.1 Add and Subtract Polynomials\",\"url\":\"https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/10-1-add-and-subtract-polynomials\",\"publisher\":\"OpenStax\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:41:57.993943+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Defines a binomial as a polynomial with exactly two terms.\"},{\"title\":\"11.6 Binomial Theorem\",\"url\":\"https://openstax.org/books/precalculus-2e/pages/11-6-binomial-theorem\",\"publisher\":\"OpenStax\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:41:57.993943+00:00\",\"supports\":\"States that the nth-power expansion of a binomial has n+1 terms and connects its coefficients with Pascal’s triangle.\"}],\"research_queries\":[\"site:mathworld.wolfram.com \\\"2\\\" \\\"only even prime\\\"\",\"site:openstax.org binomial theorem Pascal triangle coefficients two terms\"],\"notes\":\"The overview is one paragraph of 96 words. Claims about primality, geometry, binomials, and binomial expansions are tied to pages opened and read in this run.\",\"unresolved_concerns\":[],\"self_check_complete\":true}"
